00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.569
now premium salon hair care products versus
drugstore products specifically conditioners

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:06.099
there's a myth that drugstore conditioners
leave build up while salon products don't

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:11.760
but the truth is all conditioning products
leave stuff on and in the hair this is fundamental

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.969
to how they work hair is dead the only living
part is the part inside your scalp the bulb

00:03:16.969 --> 00:03:21.120
so the hair up near your scalp the bit that's
just come out of the bulb this is really nice

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.609
and neat and it looks a bit like this under
a microscope but hair grows at about 14 centimeters

00:03:25.609 --> 00:03:31.349
per year this is about 5.5 inches that's down
to around here so by the time your hair reaches

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:38.370
here it's built up about a year's worth of
damage that's 365 days of brushing washing

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:42.629
sun exposure drying it with heat straightening
it with heat and that's if you don't blast

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:47.020
it with bleach like i did little bits get
snapped off the outside of the cuticle and

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:50.859
it ends up looking a bit like this so you
can imagine the difference between how these

00:03:50.859 --> 00:03:55.670
two sections of hair feel closer to your scalp
it feels smooth the longer it is the rougher

00:03:55.670 --> 00:04:00.040
it gets and then when you have these battered
surfaces when they smash against each other

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.760
then you get more and more damage

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.519
to make something battered feel smooth again
sanding it down and wearing it down even more

00:04:06.519 --> 00:04:11.569
isn't a really good approach you have to add
stuff back into the hair to plug up the gaps

00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:16.030
and that's exactly what all conditioning treatments
do they fill in gaps not just on the surface

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:21.060
but also deeper down they fill in weaker spots
where chunks have washed off or worn away

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.610
and all of this extra reinforcement makes
hair more like the intact hair that was newer

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.940
it makes it smoother and stronger

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.520
if you look at the ingredients list of any
hair conditioner out there well almost everyone

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.320
i'm sure there's one out there that breaks
this rule there is going to be a cationic

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.180
surfactant these have names like cetrimonium
chloride behentrimonium chloride distearyldimonium

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:47.330
chloride stearamidopropyl dimethylamine and
in the acidic pH of a conditioner all of these

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.250
have a positive charge and that positive charge
is really important because that's how they

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:56.940
work when hair is wet it gets negatively charged
opposite charges attract and so these positively

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:01.680
charged bits of the cationic surfactant stick
to your negatively charged hair the scientific

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.460
term for this is adsorption and the rest of
the surfactant molecule is oily and so your

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:12.380
hair is now lubricated it smooths it out it
reduces static these cationic surfactants

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:17.460
are the default active ingredients in conditioning
products there are lots of other conditioning

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:22.780
ingredients too polyquaternium polymers hydrolyzed
proteins oils and silicones and it doesn't

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:26.960
matter if they're in high-end luxury conditioners
or if they're in those two-in-one shampoo

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.000
and conditioner products that people with
shorter hair tend to use all of these work

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:37.400
by sticking to hair so some amount of buildup
is good and necessary for any conditioner

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.610
to actually work but the amount of build up
is really tiny these gaps in your hair are

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.550
microscopic you're not going to see these
in a scrape test

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:50.380
so that brings us to the idea that a product
is bad because it weighs down your hair now

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:55.710
what exactly determines how much of a conditioning
ingredient sticks to and stays on your hair

00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:00.530
well it depends on a number of factors what
the ingredient actually is how you use the

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:05.360
product on your hair how much is in the product
what the overall formula is how you use the

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:10.370
product on your hair and what your hair is
actually like and i think this is really why

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:14.470
there's this perception that silicones can
build up on your hair and weigh it down if

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:19.610
you use the product that's suited for someone
else's hair on your hair so if you use a product

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:25.190
that's meant for coarse resistant hair on
fine porous hair then it can build up in your

